From 32582a15fb40c239c55bf864679b6e1bb6f58f15 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 13:26:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 107/206] feat(shorebird): land --compute_dd_table
 (CascadeLimiter)

The selector behind analyze_snapshot --compute_dd_table, from their private
runtime/vm/shorebird/cascade_limiter.cc: Kahn longest-path depth over the callee
edges, std::sort over the identity permutation of ALL nodes with that single
key, then a growing greedy that cuts a function when its transitive caller
closure (stopping at already-cut nodes, excluding itself) exceeds --dd_max_bytes.
Emission ascends by self_hash with a dense index.

The visit order calls the real std::sort rather than transcribing introsort.
Their comparator has exactly one key and std::sort is not stable, so the tie
order IS libc++'s trajectory; the compiler that builds analyze_snapshot resolves
<algorithm> to a sort.h whose md5 matches the copy already validated
behaviourally against the real std::sort on all 1380 positions.

Also lands the callee graph, which everything above needs and which the dump
used to omit: an edge is a b/bl whose target leaves the function. Measured
against their own `callees` field on their base.so -- 1380/1380 sets, 3629/3629
edges, nothing extra or missing -- so --shorebird now emits it.

Verification, because byte-identity with their artifact is structurally
impossible (neither analyze_snapshot reads the other's snapshots, and from their
own base.dill ours emits 1589 entries against their 1380): a two-link chain,
each link byte-exact. The reference model reproduces their --compute_dd_table
byte for byte at all seventeen budgets and on the shipped base.ddtable; our
binary reproduces that model byte for byte at the same seventeen budgets, with
the graph independently rebuilt from the ELF so the comparison is not
self-referential. verify_all.sh 14 ran / 0 failed, run_vm_tests 143 -> 154,
verify_reproduction.sh still 28/28.

Interface deviations are flagged in cascade_limiter.h rather than hidden: their
Run() carries no eligibility argument, so we encode it as an empty hash, and
Dart_ComputeDDTable's other four parameters could not be attributed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

